.TH PORTKEEPER 1 "2026-10-21" "Portkeeper @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
portkeeper \- inspect local listening ports and stop their owners
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B portkeeper list
.RB [ \-\-protocol " tcp|udp" ]
.RB [ \-\-family " ipv4|ipv6" ]
.RB [ \-\-pid " PID" ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B portkeeper inspect
.I PORT
.RB [ \-\-protocol " tcp|udp" ]
.RB [ \-\-family " ipv4|ipv6" ]
.RB [ \-\-pid " PID" ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B portkeeper stop
.I PORT
.RB [ \-\-protocol " tcp|udp" ]
.RB [ \-\-family " ipv4|ipv6" ]
.RB [ \-\-pid " PID" ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-wait " DURATION" ]
.br
.B portkeeper
.BR \-\-help " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Portkeeper queries the current Linux network and PID namespace view using
.BR ss (8).
It shows TCP listeners and bound, unconnected UDP sockets, including IPv4
and IPv6, with verified visible PIDs and process names.
Established TCP connections, bound but non-listening TCP sockets, and
connected UDP sockets are excluded.
.PP
.I PORT
is a decimal integer from 1 through 65535.
Both protocols and both address families are selected by default.
Shared sockets have separate rows for each visible owner; duplicate
descriptors for the same owner are collapsed.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List matching sockets, sorted by port, protocol, local address, PID, and inode.
An empty list succeeds.
.TP
.BI "inspect " PORT
Show matching sockets and best-effort process command lines.
Command-line data is limited to 4095 raw bytes.
NUL argument separators become spaces and trailing spaces are removed.
The result is a display string, not a shell command or argument array.
Command lines may contain secrets.
.TP
.BI "stop " PORT
Display matching owners, then require the exact answer
.BR yes .
EOF or any other answer cancels.
After confirmation, send SIGTERM to each selected owner.
Terminating a process affects all its sockets and other work, including
sockets on ports not selected by this command.
.SH OPTIONS
Options follow the command; their order relative to the port is flexible.
Repeated options and unsupported command/option combinations are usage errors.
.TP
.BI "\-\-protocol " tcp|udp
Restrict any command to TCP or UDP sockets.
.TP
.BI "\-\-family " ipv4|ipv6
Restrict any command to IPv4 or IPv6 sockets using ss's family selection.
An IPv6 dual-stack listener is selected by ipv6, even if it accepts IPv4 traffic.
The filter applies to initial discovery, stop revalidation, and final verification.
A successful verified stop only establishes the result for the selected family;
other families can still have listeners on the port.
Terminating a selected process affects all its sockets, across both families.
.TP
.BI "\-\-pid " PID
For list and inspect, show only socket rows with verified ownership by this PID.
The filter combines with protocol and family selection, in table or JSON output.
An empty list succeeds; an unmatched inspect returns status 3, including for an
exited PID or a family/protocol mismatch.
Unavailable owners are omitted but their visibility warning remains on stderr;
an empty result does not prove an inaccessible process owns no sockets.
Queries observe a snapshot and do not pin identity across separate invocations.
.I PID
must be a positive decimal integer no greater than 2147483647 for all commands.
.PP
.RS
For stop, select one verified owner of the matching port, protocol, and family.
Other owners are not signaled.
The initial table still shows all matching owners; a following message
identifies the selected PID.
An absent, exited, or non-owner PID returns status 3 when owners are visible.
Unresolved owners still cause refusal with status 5.
This option does not bypass confirmation or ownership checks.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
For stop only, validate targets and report the intended SIGTERM operation
without delivering termination signals or asking for confirmation.
Combining this option with
.B \-\-wait
does not wait.
.TP
.BI "\-\-wait " DURATION
For stop only, observe the signaled processes through their original PID
descriptors, then query matching sockets again.
Use a positive whole number of seconds
.RB ( 3 " or " 3s )
or milliseconds
.RB ( 250ms ),
up to one hour (3600s).
All targets share one monotonic deadline, starting after signal delivery.
The wait ends early when all targets have exited, including unreaped zombies.
.PP
.RS
Signal delivery, process exit, and remaining sockets are reported separately.
A still-running target gives status 8, even if it released its socket.
If all targets exited but matching sockets remain, status is 9.
With
.BR \-\-pid ,
only the selected process is monitored, but the final socket query includes
all owners matching the selected port, protocol, and family.
Remaining or replacement owners are never signaled by verification.
The final query has its own 15-second timeout; the wait duration does not
bound discovery, confirmation, or this final query.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-json
For list and inspect only, write one versioned JSON document and a newline
to standard output. Diagnostics and visibility warnings use standard error.
See JSON OUTPUT below.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show usage. Use as the sole argument, without a command.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version. Use as the sole argument, without a command.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
The top-level object contains integer
.B schema_version
(currently 1) and a
.B sockets
array. Each row contains integer
.BR port ,
string
.BR protocol " (tcp or udp),"
string
.BR local_address ,
integer-or-null
.BR pid ,
and string-or-null
.BR process .
Null means unavailable; a process name equal to a display placeholder is
still a string when available.
.PP
Inspect adds string-or-null
.B command_line
and boolean-or-null
.BR command_line_truncated .
The latter is true when raw command-line data exceeded the capture limit,
false for a complete capture, and null when the command line is unavailable.
List omits both command-line fields and does not read process arguments.
.PP
An empty result is:
.PP
.nf
{"schema_version":1,"sockets":[]}
.fi
.PP
An unmatched inspect still writes this object but exits with status 3.
Argument and discovery failures write no JSON; output errors can leave a
partial document. Check exit status before using the output.
Consumers should select fields by name and tolerate additional fields;
key order and whitespace are not part of the interface.
Names, arguments, and addresses replace control and non-ASCII bytes with
question marks. Quotes and backslashes are escaped in JSON strings.
.SH STOP SAFETY
Owner PID/descriptor claims are checked against socket inodes in
.BR /proc .
Stop pins proc directories and PID descriptors before confirmation;
signals use
.BR pidfd_send_signal (2)
instead of a reusable numeric PID.
PID 1 and Portkeeper itself are protected.
.PP
The complete matching socket/owner snapshot is collected again after
confirmation. Changes, including changes to unselected matching owners,
abort before signaling. An unresolved owner in the initial matching
snapshot refuses the entire stop, even with
.BR \-\-pid .
.PP
Process exit or socket changes can still occur immediately after the last
check. Delivery to multiple processes is not atomic: an error or interruption
can leave some targets already signaled.
Without
.BR \-\-wait ,
success only confirms SIGTERM delivery, not exit or a free port.
A process may ignore SIGTERM or a service manager may restart it.
The final socket result is a snapshot, not a port reservation or a promise
that a subsequent bind will succeed.
.SH VISIBILITY AND REQUIREMENTS
Requires Linux and iproute2 with
.B ss
on a trusted PATH. Stopping additionally requires Linux 5.3 or later with
PID descriptor operations permitted by the environment.
No unsafe PID-based fallback is used.
.PP
Visibility depends on UID, proc mount settings, namespaces, and security
policy. Unavailable owners appear as a dash and
.B <unavailable>
in the table, or null in JSON.
ss may omit inaccessible co-owners even when another owner is visible;
Portkeeper cannot certify that every owner was discovered.
Use appropriate privileges for the required visibility.
Setuid/setgid execution and namespace switching are unsupported.
Malformed results, output exceeding 16 MiB, and queries exceeding
15 seconds are rejected.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Query or dry-run succeeded, or SIGTERM delivery succeeded.
With verification, targets also exited and no matching sockets remained.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime or I/O error; a stop may be partially completed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command, option, port, or PID.
.TP
.B 3
No matching socket for inspect/stop after filters, or no requested stop owner.
.TP
.B 4
Missing, failed, or incompatible ss; or unavailable PID descriptor support.
.TP
.B 5
Insufficient permission, unresolved owner, or protected target.
.TP
.B 6
Process, socket, or owner changed. Inspect again before retrying.
.TP
.B 7
Confirmation declined or EOF.
.TP
.B 8
A signaled target was still running at the wait deadline.
.TP
.B 9
All signaled targets exited but matching sockets remained.
.TP
.B 128 + signal
Interrupted; for example, 130 for SIGINT and 143 for SIGTERM.
.PP
Statuses 5 and 6 can also occur during delivery after earlier targets
received SIGTERM. A final query failure overrides the wait result;
a delivery failure takes precedence over verification of its signaled subset.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
portkeeper list
portkeeper list \-\-family ipv4 \-\-pid 4012 \-\-json
portkeeper inspect 8080 \-\-family ipv6 \-\-pid 4012 \-\-json
portkeeper stop 8080 \-\-dry\-run
portkeeper stop 8080 \-\-pid 4012 \-\-wait 3s
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Used to find ss. Use a trusted value, especially with elevated privileges.
.TP
.B LC_ALL
Set to C for the ss child regardless of the caller's locale.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /proc/PID
Used for socket ownership, process names, command lines, and identity checks.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ss (8),
.BR pidfd_open (2),
.BR pidfd_send_signal (2)
